1
00:00:13,852 --> 00:00:14,974
Good evening, everybody.

2
00:00:15,013 --> 00:00:17,397
I trust you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:17,597 --> 00:00:20,283
I do not say happy, though I hope it has been happy.

4
00:00:20,323 --> 00:00:37,411
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambho Tassa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambho Tassa

5
00:00:38,134 --> 00:00:46,006
Namo tassa bhagavato arhato samma sambuddhassa.

6
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:51,155
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

7
00:00:54,158 --> 00:01:00,829
So we were looking at the discourse where Bhakta Gauta has come to see the Buddha.

8
00:01:01,509 --> 00:01:06,117
And just to remind you of these ten questions,

9
00:01:06,534 --> 00:01:13,769
undetermined questions or undetermined questions, known as abhyakta.

10
00:01:13,789 --> 00:01:20,882
The Buddha wouldn't answer them because when it came to himself, of course, there was always a presumption of a self.

11
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:38,094
of somebody who exists or not exists etc but here we can see some of the obsessions of the age about the world being eternal or not eternal whether it was finite or infinite space and time

12
00:01:38,546 --> 00:01:41,010
whether the body and the soul are one.

13
00:01:41,391 --> 00:01:47,281
So that's obviously a materialist position, the body and soul are one, body and mind.

14
00:01:48,763 --> 00:01:58,359
Soul here, I think, is a word that might combine for us our emotional life and thinking life, because the English word mind tends to be stuck up here.

15
00:02:01,183 --> 00:02:04,689
And, of course, this quadrilemma,

16
00:02:05,242 --> 00:02:18,161
between does the Buddha exist or not exist, then some sort of synthesis that he exists and not exists, and then this impossibility of neither exists nor not exists.

17
00:02:19,782 --> 00:02:21,906
So I'll just clear my throat.

18
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:24,890
Thank you.

19
00:02:25,390 --> 00:02:28,094
Yeah, nobody's going to get me a drink of water.

20
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:32,162
I don't know why I should be a dry throaty.

21
00:02:32,842 --> 00:02:36,687
So anyway, the Buddha won't answer any of this stuff.

22
00:02:36,706 --> 00:02:42,092
And he then, he gives his opinion of speculative views.

23
00:02:43,293 --> 00:02:53,581
A thicket of views, a wilderness of views, a contortion of views, a vacillation of views, going from this to that, doubt, a fetter of views.

24
00:02:53,602 --> 00:02:58,486
And it's beset by suffering, by vexation, by despair and by fever.

25
00:02:59,074 --> 00:03:04,387
Because to these things, you know, there can't be any objective.

26
00:03:06,031 --> 00:03:06,973
Oh, thank you.

27
00:03:06,992 --> 00:03:08,997
There can't be any objective proof.

28
00:03:09,198 --> 00:03:14,149
You know, it's the same with God or anything which is beyond physicality.

29
00:03:14,729 --> 00:03:15,632
How are you going to prove it?

30
00:03:17,062 --> 00:03:25,164
It doesn't lead to disenchantment, dispassion, cessation and to peace, to direct knowledge, to enlightenment or nibbana.

31
00:03:25,185 --> 00:03:29,115
Now a question came through about disinterest and disenchantment.

32
00:03:29,365 --> 00:03:53,466
so a disinterest um means you know you're not interested in something anymore and boredom often masquerades as disinterest but the difference is that when you're bored with something you know give it a couple of hours a couple of days a couple of weeks and you want it again but this interest really you've lost interest in it it no longer interests you

33
00:03:53,817 --> 00:04:03,729
And that can be because you become disenchanted or you've just grown out of it, as children do from, you know, snakes and ladders and tiddlywinks.

34
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:06,854
And they grow into something more sophisticated, graphs or something.

35
00:04:09,497 --> 00:04:16,786
But this disenchantment is to suddenly see through the delusion that we're suffering from.

36
00:04:17,189 --> 00:04:19,331
And it's always to do with this identity.

37
00:04:20,031 --> 00:04:21,372
What do we identify with?

38
00:04:21,572 --> 00:04:22,274
That's the question.

39
00:04:23,314 --> 00:04:26,817
And if we identify with anything, then, of course, we have a great right.

40
00:04:27,119 --> 00:04:28,720
So this is as far as we got.

41
00:04:31,442 --> 00:04:32,444
Speculative views.

42
00:04:34,346 --> 00:04:36,228
So now he's got another set of questions.

43
00:04:38,829 --> 00:04:45,677
When someone's mind is liberated thus, where does he reappear after death, you see?

44
00:04:46,331 --> 00:04:52,418
So when somebody is fully liberated, fully awakened, where does he reappear?

45
00:04:53,379 --> 00:04:57,262
So again, you see, the term reappears does not apply, Vajra.

46
00:04:58,064 --> 00:05:00,045
Well, where does he not reappear?

47
00:05:00,526 --> 00:05:04,290
He says, the term not reappear does not apply, Vajra.

48
00:05:04,850 --> 00:05:07,694
Well, does he both reappear and not reappear?

49
00:05:08,235 --> 00:05:12,019
He says, no, no, no, that just won't do, you see, does not apply.

50
00:05:12,892 --> 00:05:16,197
What about neither reappears nor does not reappear?

51
00:05:16,937 --> 00:05:21,442
And he says, no, neither reappears nor does not reappear, does not apply, Thatcher.

52
00:05:22,723 --> 00:05:31,392
Now, when Master Gautama is asked these four questions, he replies the term reappears does not apply, Thatcher.

53
00:05:31,994 --> 00:05:36,317
The term does not reappear does not apply.

54
00:05:36,338 --> 00:05:40,723
The term both reappears and does not reappear does not apply.

55
00:05:41,192 --> 00:05:45,737
and neither reappears nor does not reappear, does not apply, Vajra.

56
00:05:45,757 --> 00:05:49,942
Here I have fallen into bewilderment, Master Gautama.

57
00:05:50,322 --> 00:05:52,564
Here I have fallen into confusion.

58
00:05:53,105 --> 00:06:00,391
And the measure of confidence I had gained through previous conversation with Master Gautama has now disappeared."

59
00:06:01,492 --> 00:06:08,279
So he's completely confused by these negative responses.

60
00:06:09,728 --> 00:06:23,769
And the reason, of course, why the Buddha replies like this is that Vajragocha is always somewhere in the question, presuming somebody has reappeared or not reappeared.

61
00:06:25,673 --> 00:06:29,399
So the Buddha says, well, it's enough to cause you bewilderment, Vajra.

62
00:06:29,418 --> 00:06:31,062
It's enough to cause you confusion.

63
00:06:31,396 --> 00:06:42,567
For this Dhamma, Vacha, is profound, hard to see and hard to understand, peaceful and sublime, unattainable by mere reasoning, subtle to be experienced by the wise.

64
00:06:43,129 --> 00:06:54,360
It's hard for you to understand it when you hold another view, accept another teaching, approve of another teaching, pursue a different training and follow a different teacher.

65
00:06:55,322 --> 00:06:58,165
So I shall question you about this in return, Vacha.

66
00:06:58,745 --> 00:06:59,867
Answer as you choose.

67
00:07:00,944 --> 00:07:01,946
What do you think, Vacha?

68
00:07:02,687 --> 00:07:08,497
Suppose a fire were burning before you, would you know this fire is burning in front of me?

69
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:10,302
I would, Master Gautama.

70
00:07:11,043 --> 00:07:17,415
And if someone were to ask you, Vacha, what does this fire burning in front of you depend on?

71
00:07:17,435 --> 00:07:24,267
Being asked thus, Master Gautama, I would ask that this fire burning in front of me burns in dependence on grass and sticks.

72
00:07:25,295 --> 00:07:32,223
If that fire before you were to be extinguished, would you know this fire before me has been extinguished?

73
00:07:33,165 --> 00:07:34,305
I would, Master Gautama.

74
00:07:35,127 --> 00:07:43,156
And if someone were to ask you, Pacha, when that fire before you was extinguished, which direction did it go?

75
00:07:43,197 --> 00:07:47,302
To the east, to the west, to the north or the south?

76
00:07:47,322 --> 00:07:49,665
Being asked thus, what would your answer be?

77
00:07:50,454 --> 00:07:56,343
That does not apply, Master Gautama, the fire burned in dependence on its fuel of grass and sticks.

78
00:07:56,904 --> 00:08:04,476
And when this was used up, if it does not get any more fuel, being without fuel, it is reckoned as extinguished.

79
00:08:05,577 --> 00:08:16,394
So too, Vāca, the Tathāgata has abandoned that material form by which one describing the Tathāgata might describe him.

80
00:08:16,713 --> 00:08:43,446
so how do we describe somebody who's gone beyond any form of description so here you've got the buddha's difficulty in trying to express something which is by definition inexpressible it's ineffable it's beyond language and he goes on he says well i've cut it off at the root i've made it made it like a palm stump

81
00:08:43,898 --> 00:09:03,527
uh done away with it so that it no longer subject to future arising the tataka is liberated from reckoning you can't you can't in terms of material form he's profound immeasurable hard to fathom like the ocean and that's why these terms don't apply

82
00:09:04,182 --> 00:09:19,845
So whatever is within us, which finally liberates itself from this delusion of being this psychophysical organism, is something that simply cannot be described by any words or by any images.

83
00:09:20,405 --> 00:09:21,988
That's what the Buddha is telling him, you see.

84
00:09:22,812 --> 00:09:24,995
And then he goes through the five khandhas.

85
00:09:25,014 --> 00:09:28,599
So the first is form, the body, and then there's feelings.

86
00:09:28,639 --> 00:09:46,120
So he says the same about feelings, the same about perception and concepts and images and all that, the same about all the habits that constitute a human being, all these habits that are caused by or are maintained by our volition.

87
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:52,126
And finally, of course, the screen of consciousness upon which it all arises.

88
00:09:53,153 --> 00:09:56,701
So now we have to go to the physics of the time.

89
00:09:56,780 --> 00:10:09,828
When Westerners first came across this, the theologians and philosophers, they just presumed it was some form of subtle extinction.

90
00:10:10,315 --> 00:10:14,004
So the Buddha was simply a clever materialist.

91
00:10:14,647 --> 00:10:17,674
In the end, everything disappeared and that was the end of it.

92
00:10:18,056 --> 00:10:24,493
And one of the examples to show that was this image of the fire being extinguished.

93
00:10:25,063 --> 00:10:38,835
But the physics of the time was that the four elements, so the earth element, which is to do with extension, solidity, etc.

94
00:10:39,436 --> 00:10:43,865
The fire element, which is to do with any form of heat or cold.

95
00:10:45,466 --> 00:10:49,498
The water element, which is to with cohesiveness.

96
00:10:50,038 --> 00:10:55,434
So you can see the feel of that in water or an elastic band or something.

97
00:10:56,538 --> 00:10:58,884
And finally, of course,

98
00:10:59,184 --> 00:11:17,143
air or wind sometimes it's it's defined as wind which is movement now it is said that all these four elements are everywhere at all times in some uh in some combination or another so when this fire

99
00:11:17,443 --> 00:11:37,241
so when it was understood that when you rub two sticks together it wasn't the friction between the two sticks that was causing the heat and therefore the fire it was it was by rubbing two sticks you were calling you were calling the fire element around everywhere to come

100
00:11:37,441 --> 00:11:43,629
and be there in those sticks and grass.

101
00:11:44,289 --> 00:11:48,635
And that's why it was often used as an expression of attachment.

102
00:11:49,336 --> 00:11:58,509
So now when the fire is extinguished, the fire element simply goes into a non-grasping state.

103
00:11:59,690 --> 00:12:00,451
See, that's the point.

104
00:12:00,692 --> 00:12:03,455
So there's not a destruction of the fire element.

105
00:12:03,956 --> 00:12:06,198
There's only a destruction of this fire.

106
00:12:06,618 --> 00:12:11,907
And as I say, it's often used as an example of clinging.

107
00:12:12,769 --> 00:12:26,772
So what the Buddha is saying that there's something in us, which once it's lost its identity with this psychophysical organism, all this thing about, I think, I feel, I am the body, etc.

108
00:12:26,832 --> 00:12:28,254
When all that's gone,

109
00:12:28,740 --> 00:12:32,605
there is a release of that which knows.

110
00:12:33,006 --> 00:12:37,013
And that, of course, is your satipanya, your intuitive awareness.

111
00:12:37,413 --> 00:12:58,022
Now, when you're meditating and you've accessed that observation post within yourself and you are observing sensations coming from the body or hearing things at the ear or you're feeling things in the body or you can see, you know, images and thoughts in your mind, remember that

112
00:12:58,524 --> 00:13:05,735
unwittingly, you might say, we have pulled out of the identity with all these things.

113
00:13:06,317 --> 00:13:12,748
We're actually identifying with this observer, identifying with the feeler, the one who knows.

114
00:13:13,408 --> 00:13:23,384
So, you know, it's like in your meditation, try to make that sort of obvious to yourself, you know, by saying, well, who is the observer?

115
00:13:23,745 --> 00:13:24,886
Who is the feeler?

116
00:13:25,288 --> 00:13:29,645
But it is because it's not what is actually being felt and observed.

117
00:13:30,486 --> 00:13:46,422
And so at that point within ourselves, when we are the observer, we've only got to, as it were, jump that one more step to go beyond the idea of some body observing, some body feeling.

118
00:13:47,582 --> 00:13:56,831
And that will happen naturally with time, with the effort, with that question mark constantly in the mind.

119
00:13:58,162 --> 00:14:24,337
so that's the buddha's answer uh and he warns us that uh trying to trying to describe this is is is a fool's game which i've just done of course so so we've now come to the end of the discourse and and this is a vachagota seems to um seems to have clicked master gotama supposing there were a great sala tree not far from the village or town

120
00:14:25,144 --> 00:14:40,702
and impermanence wore away its branches and foliage, its bark and its sapwood, so that on a later occasion, being divested of branches and foliage, divested of bark and sapwood, it became pure, consisting entirely of heartwood.

121
00:14:41,764 --> 00:14:52,096
So too this discourse of Master Gautama is divested of branches and foliage, divested of bark and sapwood, and is pure, consisting entirely of heartwood.

122
00:14:52,650 --> 00:14:56,274
So in other words, he's got right down to the root of the whole problem.

123
00:14:57,475 --> 00:15:03,980
Now this is really convinced Vachagota because he says, magnificent Master Gautama, magnificent Master Gautama.

124
00:15:04,500 --> 00:15:22,336
Master Gautama has made the Dhamma clear in many ways as though he were turning upright what had been overthrown, revealing what was hidden, showing the way to one who was lost or holding up a lamp in the dark for those with eyesight to see form.

125
00:15:22,924 --> 00:15:27,894
I go to Master Gautama for refuge, to the Dharma and to the Sangha.

126
00:15:28,676 --> 00:15:37,913
From today, let Master Gautama remember me as a lay follower who has gone to him for refuge, for life.

127
00:15:38,514 --> 00:15:39,937
So he was obviously...

128
00:15:40,558 --> 00:15:49,892
He was obviously well affected by the Buddha's image or metaphor of the fire.

129
00:15:50,253 --> 00:15:51,634
He finally got it.

130
00:15:51,654 --> 00:15:53,076
So we'll see.

131
00:15:53,376 --> 00:15:59,125
We'll see tomorrow because there is a greater discourse on Vajragota, which we'll have a look at tomorrow.

132
00:15:59,846 --> 00:16:03,511
And I think this is probably happened before, but we'll see anyway.

133
00:16:03,931 --> 00:16:15,628
So I hope that the questions that Vajra got has also enlivened you and made things clear as it has done for him.

134
00:16:16,408 --> 00:16:29,187
And that you will, by your continuing practice, break through this delusion of self and attain pure enlightenment, pure liberation from suffering sooner rather than later.

135
00:16:30,809 --> 00:16:32,773
So we can begin our meditation.

